-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD-PARTY DATA LICENSES AND ATTRIBUTION NOTICES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository contains code, notebooks, and derived outputs that depend on
or were produced using the following third-party datasets and resources. The
MIT License above applies exclusively to the original source code, notebooks,
documentation, and generated synthetic/derived artifacts authored by Isaac
Maciel. It does NOT extend to, override, or relicense any third-party dataset
listed below. Users must independently comply with the terms of each dataset's
own license before accessing, downloading, or reproducing any data-dependent
results.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. PTB-XL: A Large Publicly Available Electrocardiography Dataset
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Source:     PhysioNet / Computing in Cardiology
  Version:    1.0.3
  URL:        https://physionet.org/content/ptb-xl/1.0.3/
  License:    Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
              https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

  Citation (required by CC BY 4.0):
    Wagner, P., Strodthoff, N., Bousseljot, R., Samek, W., & Schaeffter, T.
    (2022). PTB-XL, a large publicly available electrocardiography dataset
    (version 1.0.3). PhysioNet.
    https://doi.org/10.13026/kfzx-aw45

    Wagner, P., Strodthoff, N., Bousseljot, R.-D., Kreiseler, D.,
    Lunze, F. I., Samek, W., & Schaeffter, T. (2020). PTB-XL, a large
    publicly available electrocardiography dataset. Scientific Data, 7, 154.
    https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0495-6

    Goldberger, A., Amaral, L., Glass, L., Hausdorff, J., Ivanov, P. C.,
    Mark, R., ... & Stanley, H. E. (2000). PhysioBank, PhysioToolkit, and
    PhysioNet: Components of a new research resource for complex physiologic
    signals. Circulation, 101(23), e215-e220.

  Usage in this project:
    PTB-XL raw signal files (WFDB format, 100 Hz and 500 Hz) and the
    accompanying metadata CSV files (ptbxl_database.csv, scp_statements.csv)
    are used exclusively for academic research and educational purposes at
    FIAP (Faculdade de Informatica e Administracao Paulista), Sao Paulo,
    2026. Raw data files are tracked by DVC and are NOT stored in this Git
    repository. They reside exclusively in a local DVC remote on the
    developer's private machine and are never published to any public
    storage. Processed derivatives (engineered feature CSVs, spectrograms,
    tensor arrays) are similarly excluded from public distribution via
    .gitignore and .dvcignore.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. MIMIC-IV-ECG: Diagnostic Electrocardiogram Matched Subset
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Source:     PhysioNet / Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center
  URL:        https://physionet.org/content/mimic-iv-ecg/
  License:    PhysioNet Credentialed Health Data License 1.5.0
              https://physionet.org/content/mimiciv/view-license/

  IMPORTANT - CREDENTIALED ACCESS REQUIRED:
    MIMIC-IV-ECG is a restricted-access dataset. Access requires completion
    of a recognised human subjects research training course (e.g., CITI
    Program) and approval of a data use agreement through PhysioNet.
    This dataset is NOT publicly downloadable without credentials.

  Usage in this project:
    MIMIC-IV-ECG is listed as a planned Phase 2+ data source for cross-
    dataset generalisation experiments. As of Phase 1, NO MIMIC-IV-ECG
    data has been downloaded, processed, or committed to this repository
    in any form. The directory data/raw/mimic-iv-ecg/ contains only a
    .gitkeep placeholder. Any future use will strictly follow the terms
    of the PhysioNet Credentialed Health Data License and will never be
    pushed to any public repository or remote storage.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Clinical NLP Documents (SBC / SUS Guidelines and ANVISA Prescribing Info)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  This project processes a corpus of publicly available Portuguese-language
  clinical guidelines and drug prescribing information sourced from:

  - Sociedade Brasileira de Cardiologia (SBC) - Brazilian Society of
    Cardiology. Clinical guidelines published under open-access terms for
    educational and research use.
    URL: https://www.cardiol.br/

  - Sistema Unico de Saude (SUS) / Ministerio da Saude - Brazilian national
    health system. Clinical protocols and therapeutic guidelines published
    as public domain government documents.
    URL: https://www.gov.br/saude/

  - ANVISA (Agencia Nacional de Vigilancia Sanitaria) - Brazilian national
    health regulatory agency. Drug prescribing information (bulas) published
    as public regulatory documents.
    URL: https://www.gov.br/anvisa/

  Usage in this project:
    These documents are used solely for academic NLP research (text pruning,
    TF-IDF vectorisation, and construction of a clinical edge-trigger lookup
    table). Raw PDF files are tracked by DVC and are not distributed via this
    Git repository. The derived edge_trigger_lookup.json (6.41 KB) contains
    only aggregated statistical weights with no verbatim reproduction of
    source text.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GENERAL DATA ETHICS STATEMENT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All datasets used in this project were accessed and used in accordance with
their respective license terms. No patient-identifiable information has been
extracted, stored, or published at any stage of this project. The PTB-XL
dataset is fully de-identified per PhysioNet standards. Processing pipelines
were designed with data minimisation principles: only the features strictly
necessary for the research questions were computed and retained.

This project is an academic prototype developed as part of the FIAP
postgraduate AI programme (2026). It is NOT a certified medical device, has
NOT undergone clinical trials, and is NOT intended for diagnostic use in
any clinical setting. Results and metrics reported in this repository are
research outputs for educational evaluation purposes only.

